# The current task lives in a file

`.cursor/NEXT-PROMPT.md` holds the task you are working on right now. It is
written and replaced by the reviewing agent.

**Since 2026-08-23 it holds the whole ten-stage plan through to MVP, not one
round's tasks.** Do not wait for it to be replaced before continuing — work
down the stages in the order it gives, and write your notes each time you
stop. It will be replaced only when the plan itself changes.

- When Adil says **"continue"**, **"next"**, or asks what to work on, read
  `.cursor/NEXT-PROMPT.md` and do what it says.
- Treat it as the brief. It names what to build, what "done" means, and what
  not to touch.
- **Do not edit or delete it.** It is overwritten between rounds, and your
  edits would be lost and would confuse the review.
- When you finish, leave the code in a state where `npm run check` and
  `webapp/twm-app/test/acceptance.mjs` both pass, and say plainly in your reply
  what you did and what you chose not to do. Both get reviewed.

## Talking back

`.cursor/AGENT-REPLY.md` is yours. Write questions, blockers, disagreements and
the reasoning behind choices you made. The reviewer reads it at the start of
every round — roughly every twelve minutes — and answers in `NEXT-PROMPT.md`.
It is never overwritten without being read and archived first.

Use it. A round where you silently guessed at something ambiguous costs more
than a round where you asked.

Finished rounds are kept in `.cursor/prompts/` and past replies in
`.cursor/log/`. The rules in `travelers-world-map.mdc` apply to every round and
outrank the task file. **Document 5 outranks document 2** for product
requirements, including when an older prompt still cites doc 2.
